@Tutorial(time: 20) {
    @Intro(title: "Create a Single Domino") {
        You'll add a shape, material, collision shape, and physical properties to a single domino, one at a time.

        @Image(source: domino-entity-hero.png, alt: "A single red domino")
    }
    @Section(title: "Understand Entity-Component-System") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            Before writing code, let's explore the three roles that make up a domino.
            This architecture is called Entity-Component-System, or ECS.
        }
        @Steps {
            @Step {
                **Why use this architecture?** An inheritance-based design often puts shape, collision, and physics logic into a single "Domino" class.

                Different apps need different combinations. For an invisible physics floor, like the one you'll build in Chapter 4, you need physics without a visible model. An inheritance hierarchy may require more subclasses or duplicated code. ECS solves this by attaching the components you need instead of inheriting every feature.

                @Image(source: ecs-motivation.svg, alt: "Inheritance requires new classes for different combinations; ECS attaches selected components to a general entity")
            }
            @Step {
                **An Entity is the foundation of an object.** It represents one domino and provides position, orientation, scale, and parent-child relationships. Attach components to give it capabilities.

                > Note: There's no dedicated `Domino` class. Both a domino and the physics floor are entities; their components determine how they differ.

                @Image(source: ecs-entity.svg, alt: "An entity provides position, orientation, scale, and parent-child relationships")
            }
            @Step {
                **A Component describes an object's appearance or stores data needed for its behavior.**

                A domino needs a `ModelComponent` for its shape and materials, a `CollisionComponent` for its collision shape, and a `PhysicsBodyComponent` for its mass and physics mode.

                @Image(source: ecs-components.svg, alt: "A domino entity combines ModelComponent, CollisionComponent, and PhysicsBodyComponent")
            }
            @Step {
                **A System processes behavior using component data.** With the required physics components in place, RealityKit's built-in physics system calculates falling and collisions. You won't create a custom system in this tutorial.

                > Note: RealityKit provides systems for physics and rendering, so many apps don't need custom systems. Use a custom system for specialized behavior the built-in engine doesn't handle.

                @Image(source: ecs-system.svg, alt: "The built-in physics system uses component data to calculate position and rotation during falling and collisions")
            }
            @Step {
                **Choose only the components each object needs.** A domino needs a visible model and physics, but Chapter 4's invisible floor needs only collision and physics data. You can combine these without creating a separate class hierarchy for every combination.

                @Image(source: ecs-composition.svg, alt: "A domino uses model, collision, and dynamic physics; an invisible floor uses collision and static physics")
            }
        }
    }
    @Section(title: "Create the Shape and Material") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            Build the domino's appearance by creating its shape, configuring a material, and combining the two.
        }
        @Steps {
            @Step {
                **Add a domino factory function to `ARCoordinator.swift`.** Add the entire `makeDominoEntity()` function inside `ARCoordinator`. It creates a mesh 8 cm wide, 20 cm tall, and 4 cm deep, and returns a `ModelEntity`.

                @Code(name: "ARCoordinator.swift", file: "2-1-ARCoordinator-excerpt.swift", previousFile: "2-1-ARCoordinator-before.swift")
            }
            @Step {
                Review the dimensions and axes: width is 8 cm along X, height is 20 cm along Y, and depth is 4 cm along Z.

                @Image(source: domino-mesh-dimensions.svg, alt: "An 8 by 20 by 4 cm domino mesh mapped to the X, Y, and Z axes")
            }
            @Step {
                **Configure the surface material.** Create a `PhysicallyBasedMaterial` with a red `baseColor`, `roughness` of 0.4, and `metallic` of 0.

                > Note: Physically based rendering (PBR) materials describe how a surface responds to light. Adjust its base color, roughness, and metallic properties to create different materials.

                @Code(name: "ARCoordinator.swift", file: "2-2a-ARCoordinator-excerpt.swift", previousFile: "2-2a-ARCoordinator-before.swift")
            }
            @Step {
                Review how baseColor, roughness, and metallic produce a red, nonmetallic material.

                @Image(source: domino-material.svg, alt: "A red domino and the meaning of red baseColor, roughness 0.4, and metallic 0")
            }
            @Step {
                **Combine the shape and material.** Pass `materials: [material]` to the `ModelEntity` created from the mesh to finish the red domino's appearance.

                > Note: Gravity and collisions aren't enabled yet. You'll add them in the next section.

                @Code(name: "ARCoordinator.swift", file: "2-2-ARCoordinator-excerpt.swift", previousFile: "2-2-ARCoordinator-before.swift")
            }
            @Step {
                Review how the mesh and material combine into a `ModelEntity`.

                @Image(source: domino-model-entity.svg, alt: "Combining a box mesh and a red material into a ModelEntity")
            }
        }
    }
    @Section(title: "Add Collision and a Physics Body") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            What happens if these dominoes meet right now? They pass through each other: they only have an appearance.
            Two components let them collide, respond to gravity, and fall when pushed.
        }
        @Steps {
            @Step {
                **Add a collision shape.** Without one, dominoes pass through each other.

                > Note: `domino.generateCollisionShapes(recursive: true)` creates collision shapes from the mesh so the dominoes can collide.

                @Code(name: "ARCoordinator.swift", file: "2-3-ARCoordinator-excerpt.swift", previousFile: "2-3-ARCoordinator-before.swift")
            }
            @Step {
                **Add a physics body.** A domino needs to rest on the floor under gravity and fall when you push it.

                > Note: Attach `PhysicsBodyComponent` with `domino.components.set(...)` and set `mode` to `.dynamic`. A dynamic body responds to gravity and forces.

                @Code(name: "ARCoordinator.swift", file: "2-4a-ARCoordinator-excerpt.swift", previousFile: "2-4a-ARCoordinator-before.swift")
            }
            @Step {
                **Set each domino's mass to 0.7 kg.** Add a `dominoMass` constant and use it in the physics body's `massProperties`.

                > Note: `dominoMass` is the mass of one domino in kilograms. Start with 0.7 and observe how it falls when you run the app later.

                > Note: `massProperties: .init(mass: dominoMass)` specifies the mass directly. RealityKit calculates other properties, such as resistance to rotation, from the collision shape.

                @Code(name: "ARCoordinator.swift", file: "2-4-ARCoordinator-excerpt.swift", previousFile: "2-4-ARCoordinator-before.swift")
            }
            @Step {
                **RealityKit automatically simulates the completed domino.** With model, collision, and physics body components, it rests on the physics floor, falls when pushed, and collides with neighboring dominoes. RealityKit's built-in physics system handles those calculations.

                @Image(source: domino-entity-physics-flow.svg, alt: "RealityKit simulates a domino with model, collision, and physics components as it rests, falls, and hits the next domino")
            }
        }
    }
    @Section(title: "Occlude Dominoes Behind People") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            If a hand is in front of a domino, the domino should disappear behind it. Without occlusion, it renders over the hand.
            People Occlusion lets ARKit identify people in camera frames in real time and hide virtual content behind them.
        }
        @Steps {
            @Step {
                First check device support with `ARWorldTrackingConfiguration.supportsFrameSemantics(.personSegmentationWithDepth)`.

                > Note: People Occlusion requires an **A12 chip or later**, a higher requirement than Chapter 1's A9 baseline, so not every ARKit device supports it.

                @Code(name: "ARViewContainer.swift", file: "2-5a-ARViewContainer-excerpt.swift", previousFile: "2-5a-ARViewContainer-before.swift")
            }
            @Step {
                If supported, add `.personSegmentationWithDepth` to `configuration.frameSemantics`.

                > Note: Dominoes will now be occluded naturally when a person moves in front of the camera.

                @Code(name: "ARViewContainer.swift", file: "2-5-ARViewContainer-excerpt.swift", previousFile: "2-5-ARViewContainer-before.swift")
            }
            @Step {
                On the left, the domino renders over the hand without occlusion. On the right, it disappears behind the hand.

                @Image(source: people-occlusion-proportions.png, alt: "People Occlusion comparison: a domino renders over a hand on the left and behind it on the right")
            }
        }
    }
}
